# Exclude build artifacts and large directories from the Docker build context.
# Context is the monorepo root (the parent of cognee-rust/).
#
# The filename matters. This was `.dockerignore` sitting next to the Dockerfile,
# which Docker never reads: it resolves `.dockerignore` relative to the CONTEXT
# ROOT, and the context here is the monorepo root one level above this repo.
# `docker buildx build --check` confirmed it — `load .dockerignore` reported
# `transferring context: 2B`, i.e. an empty ignore list. So none of the rules
# below had ever applied. BuildKit does honour `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore`
# next to the Dockerfile, which is what this name is for.
#
# This became load-bearing rather than cosmetic once the Rust binaries moved to
# the runner: the build now runs AFTER `cargo build --release`, so a multi-GB
# `cognee-rust/target/` sits in the context on CI too, not just locally.

# Rust build artifacts, in every workspace (root, capi/, bindings/, and the
# python/ts binding trees). `cognee-rust/dist/` is deliberately NOT excluded —
# the harness stage COPYs the release binaries from it.
**/target/

# Git
**/.git/

# Python caches
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
**/.pytest_cache/

# Runtime data
**/.data_storage/
**/.cognee_system/
**/.cognee_cache/

# Node
**/node_modules/

# IDE
**/.idea/
**/.vscode/

# OS
**/.DS_Store

# Docker-in-Docker
**/docker-compose.override.yml

# Large unneeded dirs in Python repo
cognee/cognee-frontend/
cognee/.chromadb_data/
